And we can't fight this because that's what mapreduce means. Why don't you add a column to each data frame with the name of the file the record comes from? Then you can use that column as a key in the map phase and get the records grouped as you expect them  in the reduce phase

test1 = cbind("test1", test1)
test2 ...

map = function(k,v) keyval(v[[1]], v)

and turn on reduce.on.data.frame in mapreduce. Makes sense? To do it on the map side as you suggest, it's harder. If I have an idea I will let you know. There is a pretty ugly way to figure out what file each record comes from, but you are not guaranteed to see all the ones from one input file in the same mapper

There is an experimental branch, multirec-io where I am working on speed issues, starting from the readers. There is already in a much faster csv reader that reads any number of rows at once. Unrelated to your issue but if you work on csv keep an eye on it. Eventually, a generalization of your use case is one of my targets from the point of view of speed improvements, some to come in 1.3 and some to follow later (trying to work on input and map phase first and then move onto reduce and output). Split data into many smallish groups (each group fits in RAM) then apply some regular modeling or summary on each group.

Antonio

